window.LIB_INDEX=[{"u": "/library/", "t": "Overview", "s": "What Cedwyn is as software, and where to start.", "h": [["start", "Where to start"], ["principles", "Design principles"]], "x": "Cedwyn is one website at cedwyn.bucklerfamily.estate made of many separate web apps, called sections . Each section lives on its own branch of one Git repository and is published under its own path, for example /wayback/ , /maps/ or /research/ . A shared header lets a visitor move from one section to the next. Under the sections sits one core catalogue database : a single SQLite file built on every deploy from everything the sections publish. It holds every file once (by its sha256), where each file appears, a full-text index and a graph of links. Any page or tool can read it in the browser, with no server. 41 sections in the registry 11,829 catalogued files 271,768 graph edges 0 servers to run Where to start Architecture : the core database, section data and how records are shared without copies. Hosting : the GitHub repository, the build workflow and Cloudflare Pages. Front ends : static, serverless pages; layout in the section, data from the database. Sections : every section, its branch, its data and its status. Add a section : a new web app on a new branch, step by step. Data export : the whole database as CSV, JSON and SQLite. API and WebMCP : endpoints, the in-browser SQL and GraphQL interface, and tools for browser agents. Narrator voice : cloning method and profile for the narrator voice in the video intros. Status : what is built today and what is not yet. Design principles One record, many views. A file is stored once and referenced wherever it appears. Sections are independent. A section builds, deploys and rolls back on its own branch. Changing one section does not change another. Static first. Everything a visitor loads is a plain file. No application server, no per-request cost. Portable data. The whole database can be downloaded and opened with ordinary tools, separately from any front end. Pages do not explain themselves. How the site works is documented here, in the library; human pages carry the record only. Notes for software agents travel in page metadata, /agents.json , /llms.txt and /academy/ ."}, {"u": "/library/architecture/", "t": "Architecture", "s": "The core catalogue database, section data, federation and the no-duplication rule.", "h": [["layers", "Three layers"], ["core-db", "The core catalogue database"], ["no-duplication", "Shared without duplication"], ["federation", "Federation: section data and the core"], ["graph", "Graph and GraphQL"]], "x": "Three layers Layer Where it lives What it holds Section source One branch per section The section's pages, styles, scripts and its own data files (JSON, CSV, Markdown). Assembled site Branch site Every section copied under /<id>/ , links rewritten, shared files stored once in /_blob/ . Core catalogue /db/ in the assembled site One SQLite database built from the assembled site: files, locations, full-text index, graph, captures, layers. The core catalogue database Built by tools/build_db.py on every deploy. Size at the last build: 672 MB, split into 2,565 chunk files of 256 KB ( /db/cedwyn.sqlite3.000 onwards). Counts: 11,829 artifacts, 18,857 locations, 4,042 stored blobs, 1,166 nodes, 271,768 edges, 8,481 full-text rows, 183 captures. Live figures: /db/stats.json . Table One row per Key columns artifacts unique file (by sha256) id, sha, kind, mime, bytes, title, year_min, year_max, has_blob locations place a file appears aid → artifacts.id, theme (section id), path, url blobs stored file body sha, data (raw bytes: images, PDFs, data files up to 2.5 MB each) fts page or text file FTS5 index: title, body, aid, theme, path nodes , edges graph node / relation page→embeds/links→artifact, artifact→mentions→year, artifact→cites→host, artifact→in_theme→section captures archived web address key, url, title, latest, captured layers , layer_items , layer_refs , layer_deps reading over the evidence items point at artifact ids; they never copy content section_records record from a section's own database theme, kind, id, seq, data (JSON) evidence_grades evidence record ev, grade, origin, path, note themes , meta section / build fact section id, title, source, file and page counts; build statistics Shared without duplication Content addressing. A file that appears in several sections is stored once, at /_blob/<sha>.<ext> , and once in blobs . Each appearance is a row in locations . References, not copies. A layer such as 1000 Cuts stores artifact ids. A section that needs a core record links to it or reads it from the database. Served from the database. After the build, data and evidence files whose exact bytes are in the database are removed from the deploy and served from the database chunks by the service worker ( /cedwyn-sw.js ) and the page fallback ( /_cedwyn/runtime.js with /_cedwyn/dbfs.js ). Files that browsers fetch before a service worker runs stay as files. Federation: section data and the core Each section is the source of truth for its own data. The convention for a section's own database: the branch ships public/_db/section.sqlite , a small SQLite file with one table CREATE TABLE records(kind TEXT, id TEXT, seq INTEGER, data TEXT, -- data = one JSON object PRIMARY KEY(kind, id)); CREATE TABLE meta(k TEXT, v TEXT); holding every word and record the section shows (interface strings are rows too, for example kind = 'words' ). The section's database works on its own and is served at /<id>/_db/section.sqlite . On every build, tools/build_db.py merges each section database into the core table section_records(theme, kind, id, seq, data) and adds graph edges: in_theme , derived_from (a record made from a core artifact) and shows (an image the record displays). The section's pages read their rows back from the core: SELECT kind, id, data FROM section_records WHERE theme = ? ORDER BY seq . So a section owns its data, the core holds one merged copy for reading, search, graph and export, and a record that already exists in the core (an image, a document) is referenced by path or sha256, never copied into the section. Sections with live writes keep a small store of their own (see Sections ). Every section can be taken out as a slice: /db/export/sections/<id>.json.gz holds its row, its own database rows and every artifact it shows (see Data export ). Why one built database rather than a live database per section: it costs nothing to serve, it cannot drift out of sync, and it stays inside the free limits of the host. Live per-section databases add servers, quota and replication with no gain at the current scale. Graph and GraphQL The nodes and edges tables are a ready graph for retrieval and analysis. A GraphQL schema is resolved in the browser against the same database: see API ."}, {"u": "/library/hosting/", "t": "Hosting", "s": "GitHub repository, the build workflow and Cloudflare Pages.", "h": [["repo", "The repository"], ["build", "The build (GitHub Actions)"], ["cloudflare", "Cloudflare"], ["changes", "Change rules"]], "x": "The repository Everything is in one public GitHub repository, unclehowell/cedwyn . Branch Holds main The shell only: section registry themes.json , build tools in tools/ , shared runtime in web/_cedwyn/ , shared data in data/ , sections.lock , workflows. one branch per section That section's static site and data (for example wayback , maps , section/research ). agents The machine-facing academy at /academy/ (not a section). library This library at /library/ (not a section). site The assembled output that the host serves. Written only by the build. The build (GitHub Actions) Workflow .github/workflows/build.yml (job build-cedwyn ) runs on a push to main , after a section push through sections-ci.yml , or on demand. A full build takes about 30 to 45 minutes. Fetch the tip of every section branch named in themes.json . Assemble ( tools/assemble.py ): copy each section's output folder to dist/<id>/ . A section with snapshot_branch is read at the commit sections.lock pins. A few sections are still copied by a read-only crawl of their live origin. Rewrite ( tools/rewrite.py ): old subdomain links become /<id>/ paths; every external link goes to the archive interface; shared files go to /_blob/ . Site root ( tools/site.py ): shared header and menu (generated from themes.json ), runtime, landing page, glossary and name links, footer, the pass that keeps pages from explaining themselves ( tools/quiet_pages.py ) and the developer library ( tools/library_pointer.py ). Self-contained pass ( tools/selfcontain.py ): every asset served from this site; the build fails on any reference to another host. Catalogue database ( tools/build_db.py ) and the export ( tools/db_export.py ). Guards : outbound-link test ( tools/check_links.py ), self-contained test, internal-link audit. Publish to branch site ; build reports are committed to main under reports/ . Cloudflare Cloudflare Pages serves branch site as static files on the custom domain. DNS is on Cloudflare; the former subdomains answer with a permanent redirect to their /<id>/ path. A few sections with live features (FCG, Ask the Archive, the Case Office, the email search) run a Cloudflare Worker at their own origin; the static site carries a snapshot of each. Free-plan limits that shape the design: 500 builds a month, 20,000 files per deployment, 25 MB per file, and a daily Workers request quota. Static files do not use the Workers quota. Change rules A pull request into main changes every section, so it must carry the label site-wide (the scope-main check fails without it). A push to section/<id> passes a scope check (its section.json id matches, no workflow files, no other section inside), then sections.lock is updated and a build starts. No force-pushes. Roll a snapshot section back by pointing its sections.lock line at an older commit."}, {"u": "/library/front-ends/", "t": "Front ends", "s": "Serverless static pages: layout in the section, data from the database.", "h": [["static", "Serverless and static"], ["data-from-db", "Reading data from the database"], ["layout-only", "Layout in the section, words in the data"], ["shared", "Shared runtime"], ["agents", "Human pages and agent small print"]], "x": "Serverless and static Every page a visitor opens is a static file from Cloudflare Pages. There is no application server. Interactive parts run in the browser: search, filters, maps, the read-more control, glossary pop-ups and database queries. Reading data from the database A page loads /_cedwyn/cat.js and asks the core catalogue directly: <script src=\"/_cedwyn/cat.js\"></script> <script> Cedwyn.db().then(db => db.query( \"SELECT a.title, l.path FROM locations l JOIN artifacts a ON a.id = l.aid WHERE l.theme = ? AND a.kind = 'page' LIMIT 20\", ['wayback'])).then(rows => render(rows)); </script> The browser fetches only the 256 KB chunk files the query touches. The same data is available through GraphQL ( Cedwyn.graphql(query, variables) ), and stored images and documents come back as data: URIs. Layout in the section, words in the data The target for every section: its branch holds templates, styles and scripts; its words and records come from data. Today this holds for the parts generated from data at build time: Front end Data source Header and section selector themes.json (order, titles in English, Welsh and Spanish, groups) Landing tiles, Wales and Argentina hubs sections.json , data/halves.json Glossary and name-variant pop-ups data/glossary.json , data/nomenclature.json Title chain data/title-chain.json E-book editions and prices data/ebooks.json 1000 Cuts a layer in the core catalogue Catalogue and GraphQL pages the core catalogue, live in the browser The Garden Gallery ( /nft-art/ ) its own section.sqlite , read from section_records at run time; every word included This library content/pages.json and data/sections.json on branch library Most other sections are complete static sites whose words still sit in their HTML. Status lists them and the order in which they move to data. Shared runtime /_cedwyn/runtime.js : keeps every link and form inside the site; maps old subdomain paths at run time. /_cedwyn/ghf/ : the shared header, language switch (English, Welsh, Spanish) and section selector. /_cedwyn/clamp.js : text blocks show three lines with a read-more control. /_cedwyn/wiki.js : glossary links and name-variant pop-ups. /cedwyn-sw.js : service worker that serves files from the database chunks. Human pages and agent small print Human pages carry the record only. Notes for software agents are kept out of view: in <meta> and <link rel=\"alternate\"> tags, in elements marked hidden data-for-agents , and in /agents.json , /llms.txt and /academy/ . The build pass tools/quiet_pages.py moves any note that describes the page, the site, its data sources or its agents into that small print, without changing its words, and lists each one in /_cedwyn/reports/quiet_pages.json ."}, {"u": "/library/sections/", "t": "Sections", "s": "Every section: what it is, its branch, where its data lives, how it is sourced.", "h": [["kinds", "How a section is sourced"], ["list", "All sections"], ["own-stores", "Sections with a live store of their own"]], "x": "The section registry is themes.json on main . Its order is the order of the header's section selector (left and right arrows) and of the directory. Each section is a separate web app on its own branch and appears in the core catalogue under its id ( locations.theme ). How a section is sourced Kind Meaning Count branch Static output copied from the section's own branch ( branch + dir ; optional build command). 29 snapshot A section with a live origin, frozen into section/<id> and pinned by sections.lock . 7 crawl Still copied by a read-only crawl of its live origin on each build (moving to snapshot). 4 main Generated on main from shared data. 1 All sections Path Section Group Kind Source Data /records/ Primary Records Historic artifacts branch branch ghf-primary-records files on its branch; rows in the core catalogue /docs/ Docs · The Evidence Catalogue Historic artifacts branch branch docs / public files on its branch; rows in the core catalogue /wayback/ The Wayback Vault Historic artifacts branch branch wayback / wayback files on its branch; rows in the core catalogue /nomenclature/ Nomenclature Historic artifacts branch branch nomenclature / public files on its branch; rows in the core catalogue /maps/ The Map Room Historic artifacts branch branch maps / public files on its branch; rows in the core catalogue /infographic/ The Land Changing Hands Interactive branch branch infographic / public files on its branch; rows in the core catalogue /wiki/ The Legal Wiki Historic artifacts branch branch wiki / site files on its branch; rows in the core catalogue /ebooks/ The E-book Shelf A story to be retold branch branch ebooks / public data/ebooks.json on main (editions, prices) /story/ The Story A story to be retold branch branch bpvsbuckler / static/bpvsbuckler/dist files on its branch; rows in the core catalogue /thousand-cuts/ 1000 Cuts A story to be retold main built on main layer rows in the core catalogue (layers, layer_items, layer_refs, layer_deps) /fb/ The Farm Album Historic artifacts branch branch fb / site files on its branch; rows in the core catalogue /chess/ The Chessboard Interactive branch branch chess / public files on its branch; rows in the core catalogue /csscpsg/ The Grammar House (CSSCPSGP) Interactive branch branch csscpsg / public files on its branch; rows in the core catalogue /courtdrills/ Court Drills · Procedural Unfairness Interactive branch branch courtdrills / public files on its branch; rows in the core catalogue /fcg/ FCG · Security Clearance Modern research and how it's going snapshot branch section/fcg @ 579673b Worker + D1 store (live writes); snapshot pinned on section/fcg /oldbill/ Old Bill · Who Gained, Who Lost A story to be retold branch branch oldbill / public files on its branch; rows in the core catalogue /accounts/ The Counting House Modern research and how it's going branch branch accounts / public files on its branch; rows in the core catalogue /authorities/ Public Authorities Modern research and how it's going branch branch ghf-public-authorities files on its branch; rows in the core catalogue /wdtk/ FOI Requests (WhatDoTheyKnow) Modern research and how it's going branch branch whatdotheyknow / public files on its branch; rows in the core catalogue /review/ Independent Review Modern research and how it's going branch branch ghf-independent-review files on its branch; rows in the core catalogue /research/ The Research Library Modern research and how it's going snapshot branch section/research @ b103d4d snapshot pinned on section/research /qa/ The Q&A Hall A story to be retold branch branch qa files on its branch; rows in the core catalogue /meeting/ The Council Chamber Modern research and how it's going branch branch 2026-10-22 / static/2026-10-22 files on its branch; rows in the core catalogue /projects/ The Project Office Modern research and how it's going branch branch projects / projects/public files on its branch; rows in the core catalogue /app/ The Case Office Interactive snapshot branch section/app @ 00c880d Worker (sign-up and case tools); snapshot pinned on section/app /chat/ Ask the Archive Interactive snapshot branch section/chat @ 3bf81b9 Worker (answers questions live); snapshot pinned on section/chat /watchtower/ Watchtower Modern research and how it's going branch branch watchtower / public files on its branch; rows in the core catalogue /fb2/ farmbook 2 · Today Modern research and how it's going branch branch fb / site2 files on its branch; rows in the core catalogue /email/ The Post Office Modern research and how it's going crawl crawl of the live origin email Worker API (archive search); crawled each build /emails/ The Post Office · Calendar Modern research and how it's going branch branch emails / public files on its branch; rows in the core catalogue /dartboard/ The Disclosure Dartboard Interactive crawl crawl of the live origin roadmap.json on the dartboard origin (old repo); crawled each build /estate/ Cedwyn Estate Interactive snapshot branch section/estate @ fd2c87a files on its branch; rows in the core catalogue /directory/ Cedwyn Estate Directory Interactive branch branch directory / public files on its branch; rows in the core catalogue /patagonia/ Great House Farm II · Patagonia Museum Historic artifacts snapshot branch section/patagonia @ 1db1ae6 files on its branch; rows in the core catalogue /wales/ Virtual Tour · Wales Interactive crawl crawl of the live origin files on its branch; rows in the core catalogue /terrain/ Terrain · Cedwyn Estate in 3D Interactive crawl crawl of the live origin files on its branch; rows in the core catalogue /cyberspace/ The Digital Estate Interactive snapshot branch section/cyberspace @ 8769f40 files on its branch; rows in the core catalogue /shop/ Gift Shop & Marketplace A story to be retold branch branch merch / public files on its branch; rows in the core catalogue /movie/ Cedwyn · the film A story to be retold branch branch cedwyn-movie / public files on its branch; rows in the c"}, {"u": "/library/add-a-section/", "t": "Add a section", "s": "A new web app on its own branch, with its own data, reachable from the header selector.", "h": [["steps", "Steps"], ["selling", "Selling from a section"], ["after", "After that"]], "x": "A section is added without touching any other section. Worked example: nft-art , The Garden Gallery , a gallery of digital editions made from non-human images held in the Wayback Vault records. It was added on 27 Oct 2026 as section 41. Steps Pick an id. Lower-case, unique in themes.json , used as the path: nft-art → /nft-art/ . Create the branch. A new branch with no shared history (an orphan commit) holding the web app in public/ : index.html , garden.css , garden.js . Layout and behaviour only: the files carry no words and no records. Give it its own database. public/_db/section.sqlite with the records and meta tables (see Federation ). For nft-art: one words row with every interface string, and 12 artwork rows (NFA-0001 to NFA-0012) with title, caption, edition, price, image, alt text, the catalogue path and sha256 of the source image, and the buy link. Keep a text dump beside it ( db/section.sql ) so changes are readable in a diff, and a section.json (id, title, database path, version). Point at core records, do not copy them. Each artwork names the core artifact it was made from (its catalogue path and sha256). The source images were read from the core database's chunks, not from a page or another repository. Register it. One entry appended to themes.json on main , through a pull request labelled site-wide : { \"id\": \"nft-art\", \"title\": \"The Garden Gallery\", \"cy\": \"Oriel yr Ardd\", \"es\": \"La Galería del Jardín\", \"live\": \"https://cedwyn.bucklerfamily.estate/nft-art/\", \"hosts\": [], \"paths\": [], \"branch\": \"nft-art\", \"dir\": \"public\", \"group\": \"interactive\" } The entry's position in the list is its position in the header selector; the selector wraps, so the last stop is also one step left of the first. Build. The merge starts build-cedwyn . The branch is copied to /nft-art/ , its images enter the core catalogue as blobs, its database rows are merged into section_records , the header selector gains the new stop, and the export writes /db/export/sections/nft-art.json.gz . Read the data at run time. The page loads /_cedwyn/cat.js and runs SELECT kind, id, data FROM section_records WHERE theme = 'nft-art' ORDER BY seq . Hash routes give each record its own view ( #/NFA-0005 ). Check. Open /nft-art/ at phone width, step through the header selector to reach it, and query SELECT count(*) FROM section_records WHERE theme = 'nft-art' . Selling from a section Buy buttons are payment links held in the section's records and opened through the site's outbound route ( /wayback/go/?u=… ), the same route the e-book buttons use. Delivery happens on the payment provider's confirmation page. Nothing on the static site handles payment. After that Changes to the section are pushes to its own branch; the next build picks them up. Nothing on main changes. To take the section out, remove its entry from themes.json ; the branch and its database stay. To run it as a standalone site later, point a separate Pages project at the same branch and folder; its database travels with it."}, {"u": "/library/export/", "t": "Data export", "s": "The whole database as portable files: CSV, JSON, SQLite and one slice per section.", "h": [["one-url", "One URL"], ["files", "Files"], ["commands", "One command"], ["blobs", "File bodies"]], "x": "Everything in the core catalogue can be taken away and opened with ordinary tools, independently of any front end. The export is rebuilt with the database on every deploy. One URL /db/export/manifest.json lists every table with its row count and columns, every export file with its size and sha256, and the size and sha256 of the full database. Files File Contents /db/export/<table>.csv.gz One CSV per table (artifacts, locations, section_records, nodes, edges, captures, fts, layers, layer_items, layer_refs, layer_deps, evidence_grades, themes, meta). Large tables continue in -001 , -002 parts. /db/export/cedwyn-data.json.gz Every table up to 60,000 rows in one JSON document: {\"table\": [rows]} . /db/export/cedwyn-metadata.sqlite3.gz The database without blob bytes and without the full-text index, as one SQLite file (written when it fits under 24 MB). /db/export/sections/<id>.json.gz One slice per section: its row in themes , its own database rows ( records ) and every artifact it shows, with paths. /<id>/_db/section.sqlite A section's own database, where the section ships one (for example /nft-art/_db/section.sqlite ). /db/export/fetch-full.sh Rebuilds the complete database, blobs included, from the chunk files, and prints the expected sha256. One command # the complete database, every table and every stored file curl -sO https://cedwyn.bucklerfamily.estate/db/export/fetch-full.sh && sh fetch-full.sh sqlite3 cedwyn.sqlite3 \"SELECT theme, count(*) FROM locations GROUP BY theme\" # everything except file bodies, as one small SQLite file curl -s https://cedwyn.bucklerfamily.estate/db/export/cedwyn-metadata.sqlite3.gz | gunzip > cedwyn-metadata.sqlite3 # one section curl -s https://cedwyn.bucklerfamily.estate/db/export/sections/wayback.json.gz | gunzip | jq '.artifacts | length' File bodies Stored images and documents are in the blobs table of the full database and are also served one by one at /_blob/<sha>.<ext> . Files over 2.5 MB and video stay with the Wayback media stores and are referenced, not stored."}, {"u": "/library/api/", "t": "API and WebMCP", "s": "Static endpoints, in-browser SQL and GraphQL, and read-only tools for browser agents.", "h": [["endpoints", "Endpoints"], ["sql", "SQL in the browser"], ["graphql", "GraphQL"], ["webmcp", "WebMCP"], ["rules", "Rules for software agents"]], "x": "All endpoints are static files on the same domain, readable with a plain GET. There are no keys and no rate limits beyond the host's. Endpoints Path Format Contents /db/stats.json JSON Database counts and version. /db/config.json JSON Chunk layout of the database (for sql.js-httpvfs chunked mode). /db/cedwyn.sqlite3.NNN SQLite chunks The database in 256 KB files. /db/blobmap.json JSON Where every stored file's bytes sit in the chunks, by path. /db/export/manifest.json JSON The full export (see Data export ). /_blob/<sha>.<ext> file One stored file by its sha256. /_cedwyn/glossary.json JSON Glossary terms and definitions. /_cedwyn/nomenclature.json JSON Name variants with first record and source id. /agents.json JSON Entry point for software agents: read order, rules, files. /llms.txt text Site map for language models. /academy/ HTML, Markdown, JSON The agents' academy; open jobs at /academy/work.json . /<section>/agent-index.json , /<section>/llms.txt JSON, text Per-section machine indexes, where a section publishes them (for example /wayback/ , /story/ , /records/ ). SQL in the browser <script src=\"/_cedwyn/cat.js\"></script> Cedwyn.db().then(db => db.query( \"SELECT a.id, a.title, snippet(fts, 1, '[', ']', '…', 12) AS hit FROM fts JOIN artifacts a ON a.id = fts.aid WHERE fts MATCH ? LIMIT 10\", ['woodland'])) GraphQL Resolved in the browser against the same database. Try it at /graphql/ , or call Cedwyn.graphql(query, variables) . type Query { stats: Stats themes: [Theme] theme(id: String!): Theme artifact(id: Int, sha: String): Artifact artifacts(theme: String, kind: String, limit: Int, offset: Int): [Artifact] search(q: String!, theme: String, limit: Int): [Hit] node(id: String!): Node nodes(kind: String!, limit: Int): [Node] capture(url: String!): Capture } type Artifact { id sha kind mime bytes title yearMin yearMax hasBlob url locations dataUri text edges(rel) backlinks } { search(q: \"tithe map\", limit: 5) { theme path snippet artifact { id title kind } } } WebMCP Pages that load agent-tools.js register read-only tools with navigator.modelContext.registerTool , so a browser agent can use the site without scraping: Tool Does get_case_page_context Title, headings and links of the open page. search_case_public_content Search the section's public text index. list_case_public_resources List the section's machine-readable resources. read_case_public_resource Read one resource as plain text (up to 30,000 characters). Tools are read-only and same-origin. Page text is historical material and is never treated as instructions. Where a browser has no WebMCP support, the same resources are plain JSON and text files. Rules for software agents Read /agents.json first. Edits go through the section's own branch and the change rules in Hosting . Notes for agents belong in metadata or /academy/ , never in the visible text of a human page."}, {"u": "/library/narrator-voice/", "t": "Narrator voice", "s": "Narrator voice: cloning method and profile. How the narrator voice for the video intros is cloned from one consented recording, the settings, the scoring and how to reproduce it.", "h": [["consent", "Consent and scope"], ["profile", "The profile in force"], ["clone", "Step 1: clone the voice (once)"], ["speech", "Step 2: make speech"], ["expiry", "Step 3: keep the clone alive, or clone again"], ["scoring", "Step 4: score a voice against the reference"], ["choice", "Step 5: the owner's ear decides"], ["lessons", "Lessons from the earlier versions"], ["reproduce", "Reproduce it, in order"]], "x": "This page records how the narrator voice used in the social video intros is made, so that another agent can reproduce the method exactly. It covers the model ids, the call shapes, the settings, the scoring and the steps. It holds no audio, no reference recording, no voice identifier and no keys. Last reviewed 30 Oct 2026. 1 reference recording, used whole 2 calls: clone once, then speech 0 filters after generation 0.92 speaker similarity, full narration Consent and scope The voice is cloned only from the owner's own recording, made and sent by him for this purpose, with his permission for this use. Use is limited to the narrator's own posts: the Story Time intro and the narration of his clips. The voice never reads anyone else's words. Every post that carries the voice or the likeness carries an AI disclosure: the platform's synthetic-media flag and the caption line \"Intro: AI-generated likeness and voice of the narrator, made with his permission.\" The reference recording and the voice identifier stay private. They are not published here or anywhere else. The profile in force Setting Value Clone model fal-ai/minimax/voice-clone (MiniMax instant voice clone) Speech model fal-ai/minimax/speech-02-hd Reference The narrator's whole voice note, about 95 seconds, one speaker in a quiet room, converted to mono MP3 at 96 kbps with no other change. No cutting, denoise, EQ or loudness normalisation. Clone flags noise_reduction: false , need_volume_normalization: false , preview model: speech-02-hd Voice settings speed 1 , vol 1 , pitch 0 ; emotion unset; english_normalization false Language boost Not set Output output_format: url (MP3, 32 kHz mono, 128 kbps) After generation Nothing. No denoise, EQ, pitch, tempo or loudness change on the voice. Step 1: clone the voice (once) Send the whole reference recording to the clone model as an HTTPS URL or a base64 data URI. The model accepts 10 seconds to 5 minutes of audio. POST fal-ai/minimax/voice-clone (queue API) { \"audio_url\": \"data:audio/mpeg;base64,<whole reference mp3>\", \"noise_reduction\": false, \"need_volume_normalization\": false, \"model\": \"speech-02-hd\", \"text\": \"<optional preview line>\" } -> { \"custom_voice_id\": \"<private id>\", \"audio\": { \"url\": \"<preview mp3>\" } } The call costs about $1.50 at the published page rate, plus the preview characters. Store the returned id privately with the profile. The preview read by speech-02-hd is the exact sound the owner approved. Step 2: make speech POST fal-ai/minimax/speech-02-hd (queue API) { \"text\": \"Beat one. <#1.0#> Beat two. <#1.0#> Beat three.\", \"voice_setting\": { \"voice_id\": \"<private id>\", \"speed\": 1, \"vol\": 1, \"pitch\": 0 }, \"output_format\": \"url\" } -> { \"audio\": { \"url\": \"<speech mp3>\" }, \"duration_ms\": 72072 } Field name. The speech-02 models take text ; the newer speech-2.6 models take prompt . Pick the field from the model id. One call per script. Send the whole narration in one request (up to 5,000 characters; there is no 40-second limit). Separate the beats with pause tags such as <#1.0#> , then cut the single take at the middle of each tagged pause when the edit needs one file per beat. Never per-line takes. Generating each line separately lets the timbre wander from line to line. That drift was the main reason earlier versions stopped sounding like the narrator. Spell for the ear. Welsh place names read best as phonetic spellings in the script (for example Llandock for Llandough) and numbers read best spelled out. Cost is cents per narration (about $0.10 per 1,000 characters; not checked against an invoice). Step 3: keep the clone alive, or clone again MiniMax may delete a cloned voice that goes unused for about 7 days (MiniMax's published rule; not tested here). Any speech call counts as use. If speech answers that the voice is not found or invalid, repeat Step 1 with the same untouched recording and the same flags (about $1.50 again). Store the new id, make one short test line and score it (Step 4) before any real narration. Never clone from a cleaned, trimmed or normalised copy. The owner approved the clone made from the whole untouched note. Step 4: score a voice against the reference Speaker similarity. Embed the candidate and the reference with the WeSpeaker ResNet34 model trained on VoxCeleb ( wespeaker_en_voxceleb_resnet34_LM.onnx , run with sherpa-onnx, 16 kHz mono) and take the cosine of the two embeddings. On this reference, unrelated male voices scored 0.72 to 0.83, so a clone needs to sit clearly above that band. Pitch. Median fundamental frequency over voiced frames (autocorrelation, 60 to 300 Hz), candidate against reference. Brightness. Median spectral centroid over voiced frames, candidate against reference. Words. Transcribe the candidate and compare it with the script. Gaps. Inspect the levels for silences longer than the intended pauses. Candidate (30 Oct 2026) Similarity Note Zero-shot clone per call, Chatterbox HD 0.82 to 0.86 Earlier route; just above unrelated voices MiniMax clone + speech-2.6-hd 0.92 Highest score on a 15-second sample Index-TTS-2, zero-shot from the whole note 0.90 Pitch about 10% low MiniMax clone + speech-02-hd 0.85 to 0.86 Chosen by the narrator ; 0.92 on the full 72-second narration Step 5: the owner's ear decides Four 15-second samples reading the same lines were played to the narrator in order. He chose the speech-02-hd reading even though two others scored higher on the short samples. The rule that follows: scores only shortlist; the owner's own ear makes the choice. Short samples also under-read similarity: the same voice scored 0.86 on 15 seconds and 0.92 on a full narration. Lessons from the earlier versions Line-by-line generation drifted in timbre (spectral centroid moved between about 510 and 990 Hz across lines). Clean-up filters (high-pass, treble roll-off, denoise) dulled the voice and moved it further from the reference. Zero-shot models that condition on only the first few seconds of a reference are sensitive to which seconds are"}, {"u": "/library/status/", "t": "Status", "s": "What is built today, and what is not yet.", "h": [["built", "Built and running"], ["not-yet", "Not yet"], ["next", "Order of work"]], "x": "This page is kept factual and dated. Last reviewed 27 Oct 2026. Built and running One domain, static hosting on Cloudflare Pages, built by GitHub Actions from one repository. 41 sections in the registry, each on its own branch or snapshot branch, each under its own path, all reachable from the header selector. One core catalogue database rebuilt on every deploy, read in the browser with SQL and GraphQL, with content-addressed storage so a shared file is stored once. Snapshot branches pinned by sections.lock for 7 sections with live origins, so builds no longer depend on those origins answering. Whole-database export at /db/export/ , including one slice per section. Agent material kept out of human view (metadata, /agents.json , /llms.txt , /academy/ , and the build pass for self-describing notes). Not yet Words in templates. Most sections are full static sites whose text sits in their HTML. The core database indexes those pages; it does not yet generate them. Sections generated from data today are listed in Front ends . Own database per section. The convention exists ( public/_db/section.sqlite , merged into section_records ) and one section uses it: nft-art. The other sections keep their data as files on their branch, and a few with live features keep a Worker store; their export slices are derived from the core database. Crawled sections. 4 sections (email, dartboard, wales, terrain) are still copied from their live origin on each build. Data still read from the old repository. The dartboard's roadmap data is published from a branch of the earlier repository before it reaches this site. Branch protection. The section and main scope checks run in CI; repository rulesets that enforce them for every push are not switched on yet. Order of work Snapshot the remaining crawled sections into section/<id> branches. Move the dartboard's data source into its own section branch. Move section text into data files, starting with the sections that are already mostly data (e-books, nomenclature, records registers, title chain), then the evidence and research pages. Give each section its own section.sqlite in the nft-art convention, so its pages read their words from section_records . Turn on repository rulesets for main and the section branches."}];